I had thought about adopting since I was  a teenager I remember thinking like no  child should ever grow up in an  orphanage the difference between growing  up with a family versus growing up  without a family changes the course of a  human beings life  to become adoptive parents there's a  major process of interviews of  background checks of making sure your  home is safe for your children  I remember a social worker saying to me  look we want these kids to have families  and we're trying to opt you in not opt  you out so it's not that they're looking  for people with perfect lives they're  looking for people who are committed to  helping a child  the first thing we did we went down then  I was essentially see if this would be  the sort of place we would want to adopt  fraud as we got to know the director and  she introduced us to this little boy  named Kim BAE and said I think I know  who you guys you know need to meet and I  distinctly remember it was nap time and  so we walked around the corner into his  bedroom when he's laying in his crib and  he's laying on his back wide-awake this  little guy huge smile on his face I kid  you not just this beaming smile on his  face and just kind of quiet and looking  up at us and just you're just just kind  of beaming this sense of baby joy we're  like what kid exudes this kind of  positive energy she's like this is your  little guy like oh my god you're right  this is our son when you birth a child  you meet the child that day and it's  this beautiful moment and then you go  home and you have the child with you but  in this case we meet the child and it's  beautiful and we're excited and then we  go home and he still lives somewhere  else that was really difficult and you  know then we go home and we just  continue submitting paperwork we had to  go down for visits sometimes just to  sign things in a courthouse I was really  just down there for a casual visit I was  just checking on paperwork getting to  hang out with kambei a little bit and  the earthquake happened and it was it  was insane it was like it was like being  on a blanket that someone was trying to  shake off we survived we were all okay  many people in the country did not fare  as well it was a really devastating day  for their country he and the other  orphans were literally sleeping on the  ground for dry boy was the most  difficult aspect of that adoption we  weren't really sure what was going to  happen to him we weren't really sure  what would happen with the adoption  I really became dedicated to trying to  get our son and all of the kids that  were in the same situation home to their  families because there were hundreds of  children in the same scenario who had  adoptions approved and were literally  just waiting on a signature to be able  to come home to their families I started  working with some of our state  representatives and advocating with them  and with the Haitian government to  figure out a plan to grant humanitarian  parole to these kids the ones who were  already matched the ones whose paperwork  was just you know waiting for someone to  approve it and a week later through a  partnership with the US government and a  Haitian government they did grant  humanitarian parole and refugee status  for those children it was actually a  bill in Congress that was passed called  help for Haiti and it granted  humanitarian parole for I believe 800  children who were in situations like our  Sun can bake hopped onto a military jet  with all the other orphans and flown to  the US without us I was on the airplane  to Orlando found out that he was there  at a different Airport so somehow got  over to the other airport  finally at 1:00 in the morning 2:00 in  the morning they started bringing one or  two kids out at a time so at this point  it's been three years since I first met  Kimbe eventually somebody sees him  coming it says mark Kimba is coming out  and so I ran over to this gate area and  the officer who carried him out hands  him to me and Kim be a just melts in my  arms it's he's exhausted emotionally  exhausted physically exhausted  physically and emotionally exhausted I  just want to embrace him and hold him  after all these years and after all this  trauma of the earthquake I'm just so  thankful that he was there and the  officer gave me this paper and said hang  in there congratulations it was very  affirming and wonderful and it took the  visa and held my son and just didn't  want to let him go  just held him for so long handbag had  three siblings at home that had been  waiting for years for him to come home  we got balloons and we got signs and we  had lots of family and friends who'd  been waiting as well finally you know we  see mark and Kambei coming out and the  kids just ran and hugged him and we were  all so excited to finally be together as  a family  Wow
 

